# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

## Summary
Successfully implemented Vercel Speed Insights for the UMBC Food Radar project following the HTML framework guidelines.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
- **web_scrapper/index.html** - Added Speed Insights tracking scripts

## Implementation Details

Since this is a static HTML/Python project without a JavaScript framework, I implemented the HTML-based approach for Speed Insights:

1. Added the Speed Insights initialization script before the closing `</body>` tag
2. The implementation includes:
   - `window.si` initialization function to queue Speed Insights calls
   - Deferred loading of `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js`

The scripts were placed after the main `script.js` and before the closing body tag to ensure proper page load performance tracking.

## Technical Notes

- **Framework**: HTML (static site)
- **No package dependencies**: The HTML implementation doesn't require installing `@vercel/speed-insights` npm package
- **Vercel integration**: The `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js` route will be automatically available after the next deployment to Vercel
- **Existing Analytics**: The project already has Vercel Web Analytics installed (visible in the `<head>` section), and Speed Insights has been added alongside it

## Next Steps for Deployment

1. Enable Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard:
   - Go to Project Settings → Speed Insights
   - Click "Enable" to activate the feature
   
2. Deploy the updated code to Vercel:
   ```bash
   vercel deploy
   ```

3. After deployment, verify the Speed Insights script is loaded by:
   - Checking for `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js` in the page source
   - Viewing the Speed Insights tab in the Vercel dashboard after users visit the site

## Why This Approach?

The HTML script-based implementation is the recommended approach for static HTML sites and requires no build process or package installation. It provides the same performance tracking capabilities as the framework-specific packages while maintaining the simplicity of the current static site setup.
